WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:04.704
A few months ago we got this question
from one of our subscribers, Gaurav.

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:07.140
When I was a kid, one of my most favorite

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.942
things to do was to visit a zoo.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.944
But as I grew older, I came across evidence

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:13.847
to the fact that animals in zoos 
often suffer from boredom.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.182
Was the younger version of me wrong?

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.218
I wanted to dig into 
Gaurav's question 

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:20.620
in part because as a parent of 
a young toddler

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.289
zoos are very much a part of 
my everyday life.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:26.126
Going to the zoo clearly makes
my kid happy.

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:29.662
And most zoos market themselves 
as a force for conservation

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:32.599
protecting endangered species
and making us humans

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.801
better stewards of the planet.

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:37.771
But then there's that evidence 
Gaurav mentioned.

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:41.074
Videos showing what zoologist 
call stereotypies:

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:44.210
Repetitive behaviors like hair pulling,
tongue flicking

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.248
bobbing and swaying that indicate 
under-stimulation and stress.

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:51.651
All of this left me with a couple questions:

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:55.688
What are zoos doing for humans, 
and for animals?

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:59.993
And are they doing 
what we want them to?

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:07.267
My first call was to Susan Clayton, 
a conservation psychologist

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:10.570
whose job is basically to go to the zoo 
and spy on people.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.972
We watch people and we listen to people

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:15.408
and, you know, pay attention to what they say.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:18.812
Parents will use that as an opportunity
to talk to their children

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:23.616
about how it's important to try and 
keep species from going extinct.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.754
But something much more concrete 
and more simple also happens.

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:31.591
Susan and her colleagues noticed that
around primates in particular

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:35.295
visitors will frequently imagine themselves 
in the animals' place.

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:38.264
They might speak as if they were the animal.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.233
"What are these people looking at me for?"

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:44.704
So you are temporarily imagining what it 
would be like to be the animal.

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:48.808
That does open the door to a 
greater empathy and concern.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:52.112
Susan is describing an expansion 
of what philosophers

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.380
call our circle of empathy.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.118
We might start by only caring about 
our family and close friends

00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:00.887
but we can extend that circle 
to include other people

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:02.989
and even other species.

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:06.860
I was just wondering if that kind of resonates 
with you as far as thinking back

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.062
to your experiences in zoos growing up?

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:10.230
Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:13.266
Sometimes they would even allow us to feed giraffes.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:16.803
We were allowed to touch some 
snakes and things like that.

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:19.105
I used to love going back to that zoo

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:22.675
so that they can create this bond 
and this empathy with animals.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:26.112
So maybe zoos help us feel 
more empathy for animals.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.781
But is that enough?

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.785
Emma Marris is a science writer

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.388
who spends a good chunk of her book, 
Wild Souls

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.757
trying to answer this question.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:42.295
Do the benefits of zoos to wild animals 
and to conservation

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:45.165
outweigh any minimal amount of discomfort

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:49.002
or unhappiness that the animals 
in the zoo might experience?

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:50.470
Talking with Emma convinced me that 

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.472
instead of just looking for pros and cons

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:54.874
I should look for evidence for two claims:

00:02:55.775 --> 00:03:00.413
That keeping animals in a zoo somehow 
helps wild populations thrive.

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:03.183
And that visiting them has 
a measurable impact

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:05.852
on human attitudes about conservation.

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:08.221
Lucky for me

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:11.724
Emma had already spent a lot of time 
looking into this first question.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:15.595
The idea that zoo animals are 
a sort of backup or reserve.

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.697
That sounds reasonable, right?

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:20.233
Basically, if a species was about to go extinct

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:23.570
we could replenish their numbers 
with these reserve animals

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.905
who had spent their lives in zoos.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:28.274
The tricky part about that 
is that for a lot of species

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:31.311
especially the bigger, more 
cognitively complex species

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:34.113
putting them back out into the wild 
is incredibly difficult.

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:36.849
The key to knowing which ones 
can actually be released

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.085
is a concept called 
cognitive complexity.

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:41.988
A good way to think about it is 
to look at how long it takes

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:44.757
for a particular species 
to learn skills in the wild.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:48.261
Orangutans stay with their mothers 
for up to 9 years.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:51.631
It takes elephants the same amount of time 
to learn how to function

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:54.901
as members of one of the most complex
social groups in the animal kingdom.

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:57.804
Orcas stay with their mothers until age 13.

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:02.141
For lions, polar bears, and gorillas, 
it's at least 2 years.

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:06.279
Once you realize how much these 
cognitively complex animals

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:08.348
have to learn to survive in the wild

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:10.450
it makes sense why the ones 
you see in zoos

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.485
could never join their wild cousins.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.488
This idea of cognitive complexity

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:18.558
was just really game changing 
for me as far as

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:25.098
understanding why so few zoo animals 
are able to be out in the wild.

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:29.235
When you were talking about this, 
I had a few examples in my head.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.704
I remember seeing the footage of a 

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:34.474
baby gazelle that cheetahs often hunt.

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:38.111
So the mother would capture 
the baby gazelle 

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:43.116
and let her cubs, you know, go out 
and finish it, like kill it.

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.818
This is very hard to replicate in a zoo.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.521
For comparison, the California Condor

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:51.891
only needs 180 days to learn 
how to survive after hatching.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:54.360
And a program to save them from extinction 

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.996
by breeding them in zoos and
releasing them in the wild

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:58.931
has been a huge success.

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:01.968
But these programs are incredibly rare.

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:06.172
According to the Association for 
Zoos and Aquariums website

00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:11.044
there are approximately 8700 species
represented in zoos around the world

00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:14.681
and just 117 reintroduction programs.

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:17.950
This sort of notion that someday
they're going to be like

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.552
"Oh, we ran out of elephants."

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:20.620
"Let's get all the ones from the zoo

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.955
and just pop them out into Africa and Asia."

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:24.824
Like, that's not going to happen.

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:26.326
They're never going to let them out.

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:29.162
They're going to die in those zoos.

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:32.198
For cognitively complex animals

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:34.300
it's really hard for me to see the benefits

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.803
and the drawbacks seem pretty obvious.

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:40.740
But what about the second item on the list?

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.910
Does visiting the zoo make us 
more conservation minded?

00:05:44.911 --> 00:05:46.713
One study gave visitors a survey

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:49.782
when they first got to the zoo and 
asked them to write down actions

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:53.019
they could think of that would help
save animal species,

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:56.723
then have them do the same thing 
after they'd visited the exhibits.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.525
Slightly more people mentioned recycling

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:02.028
responsible purchasing 
and diet choices.

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:07.934
But the share of visitors who mentioned 
habitat protection actually decreased.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:12.572
So I really didn't find much evidence 
that zoos in their current form

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:14.407
do much to help wild populations

00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:17.009
or change people's attitudes and behavior.

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:21.881
But what if we could imagine
a different kind of zoo?

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:24.751
My initial question was:

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:28.154
Do the benefits of zoos outweigh its cons?

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:30.390
But now, after listening to this 

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.692
and the evidence that you have provided

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:36.496
now I feel that we must assess 
each species individually.

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:40.466
If zoos stopped breeding 
cognitively complex animals 

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:41.667
for captivity

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:44.670
and then release those they have 
to wildlife sanctuaries

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.706
like this one in Tennessee

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:48.541
they could devote more time and space

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:52.712
to threatened species who can successfully 
be released into the wild.

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:57.316
Sometimes these are turtles or fish 
or freshwater mussels, right?

00:06:57.316 --> 00:07:01.387
Like these are not necessarily 
your big blockbuster mammals.

00:07:01.888 --> 00:07:03.890
To satisfy our very real desire 

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.759
to see and hear and connect 
with non-human creatures,

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:11.431
we might need to extend that circle of empathy 
in a different direction.

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:16.235
So we see squirrels or robins 
or pigeons, and we're like meh.

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:19.672
But actually, the more I learn about
these animals and insects

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:22.141
and the plants that are in the city, 
the street trees,

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:24.477
the more fascinating I find them.

00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:29.582
Do you think we could design zoo experiences 
that people would want

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:32.985
that are based on these different species?

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:36.456
I used to love going to zoos 
and seeing the big animals.

00:07:36.456 --> 00:07:39.525
I mean, not having them around 
would be kind of sad

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:44.630
for the younger version of myself,
but I think I have changed

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.266
and that's because of education.

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:50.269
And I think people can also change if we...

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:52.972
if we educate them.

00:07:55.163 --> 00:07:56.832
Thank you so much for watching.

00:07:56.832 --> 00:08:00.526
I really enjoyed digging into this question
about zoos with Gaurav. 

00:08:00.526 --> 00:08:01.999
And if you haven't watched it already 

00:08:01.999 --> 00:08:05.590
definitely go watch Joss's video 
about unions in the US. 

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.222
That one was inspired by another question 
from a subscriber, Cameron.

00:08:09.222 --> 00:08:12.201
We're really excited to make a 
whole series of videos like this

00:08:12.201 --> 00:08:14.609
 videos driven by your questions.

00:08:14.609 --> 00:08:16.609
So, if you're up for going on camera and

00:08:16.609 --> 00:08:18.337
being a part of a Vox video

00:08:18.337 --> 00:08:19.505
hit us up!

00:08:19.505 --> 00:08:20.503
We'll get some answers.

00:08:20.503 --> 00:08:23.182
Link to the submission form
